{"custom_id": "3459b493d881ff1e66f4857132e2487b88c003e71670c0ef", "meta": {"loop": "topk4L01", "task": "infer_discrepancy_patterns_4", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "prompt_sha256": "4c59631202fbe7edcefb95f8d9982e9aa24c01d1632f95d9a54727064157a17a", "prompt_chars": 10741}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_01PKEHo3ZkuLc1L2HzZ2qszB", "saved_utc": "2026-10-22T09:51:25+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011CgH4dDMuCSLWccsXy8dKp", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "**Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis**\n\n- **Contrast:** The discrepancy spans are clinical-feature or pathogenic-state phrases (\"abnormal retinal vascular development,\" \"deficiency of norrin,\" \"spasticity\"). The true positives either name a disease directly (*Coats disease*, *diastrophic dysplasia*, *Sjogren-Larsson syndrome*) or use a disease-denoting head noun (*cancer*, *ichthyosis*). The discrepancies are not disease names and have no disease-marking head.\n- **Isolate Trigger:** All three discrepancies sit in a disease-defining or causal predicate frame: \"is characterized by\" (Ex. 1, Ex. 3) and \"results in\" (Ex. 2). The head noun denotes a process, state, or symptom (*development*, *deficiency*, *spasticity*), not a disease name. The model appears to read these as descriptive narrative and assigns O.\n- **Hypothesis Check:** The frame alone does not explain everything. *Ichthyosis* sits in the same \"characterized by\" list (Ex. 3) and is correctly tagged DiseaseClass. The model seems to rely partly on lexical disease morphology (*-osis*). So the refined trigger is: a sign, state, or mechanism head inside a disease-characterizing frame. Gold annotations are also somewhat inconsistent: *mental retardation* in the same list is not annotated.\n- **Contrast with TP 1303173:** \"G6PD deficiency\" is SpecificDisease. \"Deficiency\" is a disease-denoting state noun when it names the condition, so the model should not treat \"deficiency\" as non-entity by default.\n\n**Step 2: Formulate Pattern**\n\n- **Pattern Insight: Disease-defining manifestations and pathogenic states labeled as DiseaseClass**\n- **Confusion Trigger:** A noun phrase whose head denotes a clinical sign, symptom, or pathogenic state (*development*, *deficiency*, *spasticity*), appearing as the complement of a disease-characterizing or causal predicate (\"characterized by,\" \"results in\"), with no disease-name token. The model reads it as descriptive narrative and returns O.\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** The true positives avoid this trigger because their DiseaseClass or SpecificDisease spans are either proper disease names (*Coats disease*, *Sjogren-Larsson syndrome*) or disease-category nouns (*cancer*, *ichthyosis*) that the model recognizes lexically. Modifiers such as \"ovarian cancer\" in \"ovarian cancer cases\" are attributive and are not affected by a characterization frame.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Label a span as DiseaseClass when all of the following hold:\n  1. It is the complement of a disease-defining or causal predicate (\"characterized by,\" \"results in,\" \"secondary to,\" \"caused by\") in a sentence whose subject is a named disease.\n  2. Its head noun denotes a pathological state, deficiency, abnormality, or clinical sign (not a process, agent, or gene product).\n  3. It describes a feature that defines the disease class rather than a separate event or finding.\n\n  If the head is a recognized disease term (*-osis*, *syndrome*, *dysplasia*), use the standard SpecificDisease or DiseaseClass rule. Attributive uses such as \"X deficient\" should remain Modifier, and \"X deficiency\" as a noun naming the condition should be SpecificDisease.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_